diagnosis_text:
Location C’s LAN and router are operating normally. Hosts PC11–PC14 are on 192.168.200.0/24 with default gateway 192.168.200.1 (router4 eth0). Direct evidence: from PC11/PC12/PC13 we can ping the gateway 192.168.200.1 with 0% loss and low RTTs. Router4’s ARP table shows active entries for 192.168.200.11–.14, and router4 can ping PC11. Upstream routing is also healthy: PC11/PC12/PC13 can reach a remote site host (172.16.10.4) with 0% loss; router4 has a valid default route via 172.25.1.2 on eth5 and has learned remote prefixes via RIP. Layer-2 switching at Location C is fine: switch4 and access switches have all relevant ports up and forwarding.

Mechanism: If the LAN or gateway were down, ARP resolution and local pings would fail. Instead, successful ARP on router4 and bidirectional pings confirm L2/L3 connectivity. Remote reachability further proves WAN routing is intact.

Localization: Location C subnet 192.168.200.0/24 behind router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1), with uplinks via router4 eth5 (172.25.1.1) to router3.

Root cause: No active network fault found; the earlier outage was transient or endpoint-specific and has cleared by the time of investigation.

Corrective fix: No network changes needed. If users still report issues, have them re-check local NIC/firewall settings or restart affected PCs. Monitoring is recommended, but current connectivity from PC11–PC14 to the gateway and to other sites is confirmed operational. === END ===